{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-e48c7fd3-1428-4e2d-8998-1845fca758c6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-ad9f5c63dceedad6","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-si_ko20p/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E003.md","content":"# Investigation: artifact traffic routing mechanism for the MLflow split-server topology\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation decides how the MLflow operator should configure artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated artifacts-only instance in the split-server topology. It determines whether gateway-level path-based HTTPRoute routing suffices, or whether the main tracking server requires a configuration change to delegate artifact operations to the dedicated instance. The deliverable is a decision record that shapes the operator's HTTPRoute design and determines whether a tracking-server delegation change is needed.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Evaluate gateway-level path-based routing of artifact API traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance via Gateway API HTTPRoute resources (host/path matching, backend references), consistent with the operator's existing conditional Gateway API integration.\n- Determine whether the main tracking server needs a configuration change to delegate artifact operations to a dedicated instance, or whether routing alone suffices — based on how clients address artifact endpoints and how the tracking server serves artifacts today.\n- Document the implications for HTTPRoute design and for the main tracking deployment's configuration.\n- Classify the outcome into one of two bounded outcomes:\n  - **Outcome A**: gateway path-based routing suffices; no tracking server change is needed.\n  - **Outcome B**: a tracking-server configuration change to delegate artifact operations is required (conditional downstream work).\n- Produce a decision record with the selected mechanism and its design implications.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The decision record answers, with evidence from the tracking server's artifact endpoint structure and client behavior, whether server-side delegation is required for artifact traffic to reach the dedicated instance.\n- HTTPRoute design implications (path matching, backend targets, conditional creation based on runtime CRD discovery) are documented.\n- The outcome is classified as one of the bounded outcomes, and the downstream plan is updated accordingly (operator routing design confirmed, or the conditional tracking-server delegation branch activated).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\nEnables HLR-5 and HLR-6 (P1: operator deploys the dedicated artifacts-only instance and routes artifact traffic to it): the selected mechanism determines the routing design of the operator split-server topology work.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":67376,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-85f574c21f19b836","agent_id":"a294f4e0cbc229ff0","session_id":"93c9eca1-ec44-4ff5-8343-c9e3bc7bdf0f","uuid":"83a61aef-c950-444e-9d4d-69d323e91b0d","timestamp":"2026-10-26T17:35:35.749Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8009"}
