{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-7e33441c-436e-4338-8baa-c09c83b2a8f5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-97504a815a92963c","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8009-si_ko20p/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8009-E004.md","content":"# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic makes the MLflow tracking server's `--artifacts-only` mode composable with the existing `--enable-workspaces` flag, so the artifacts-only server initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests against tenant boundaries. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`, validates each artifact request against workspace membership, denies unauthorized requests, and fails closed when the workspace store is unreachable. Deployments that do not set `--enable-workspaces` retain the current artifacts-only behavior. This epic depends on the resolution of the workspace-aware artifacts-only feasibility questions (initialization approach, artifact path resolution) and on the plugin workspace-resolution capability being verified for the artifacts-only context.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Allow `--artifacts-only` to be combined with `--enable-workspaces` without a new server flag: when both are set, require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` (the server fails to start with a clear error if it is missing) and initialize only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests. Tracking stores and model registry stores stay uninitialized; non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n- For each artifact request, resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment (artifacts are stored under experiment-scoped paths, e.g. `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure — without prescribing Kubernetes API mechanics. Initial artifact path resolution scope: the S3 artifact store (the standard RHOAI configuration); limitations for non-standard artifact roots are documented.\n- Deny requests that fail workspace validation with HTTP 403 and an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions; deny requests with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable when the workspace store is unreachable (fail-closed).\n- Preserve current behavior when either flag is absent: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` serves artifacts without workspace validation; single-tenant deployments and existing API contracts, CLI arguments, and configuration keys are unchanged in meaning.\n- Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs (P2 requirement, bundled here because the denial log entry is written in the same artifact-request validation handler).\n- Measure the per-request latency overhead of workspace validation and apply short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions consistent with the tracking server's existing auth-path caching patterns if the measurement warrants it; otherwise document the deferral rationale.\n- Update incidental documentation (flag usage, limitations for non-standard artifact roots) that falls out of this change.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given the artifacts-only instance started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://` and a user who is a member of workspace A, when the user requests an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A, then the artifact is served — measured by HTTP 200 with correct artifact content.\n- Given the workspace-aware artifacts-only instance and a user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B, when the user requests an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B, then the request is denied — measured by HTTP 403 with an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n- Given `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, when any authenticated user requests an artifact, then the artifact is served without workspace validation — measured by HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership (current behavior preserved).\n- Given the workspace-aware artifacts-only instance running with the workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable, when a user requests an artifact, then the request is denied — measured by HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable (fail-closed).\n- The server fails to start with a clear error when `--enable-workspaces` is combined with `--artifacts-only` without an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`.\n- No existing API contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys are removed or changed in meaning.\n- Workspace access denials in artifact serving are recorded in the server logs.\n- The per-request workspace validation overhead is measured, and caching is either implemented or explicitly deferred with a recorded rationale.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` — this epic.\n- HLR-2 (P0): artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 — this epic.\n- HLR-3 (P0): fail-closed when the workspace store is unreachable — this epic.\n- HLR-4 (P0): single-tenant / non-workspace deployments retain current behavior — this epic.\n- HLR-8 (P2, bundled): audit logging for workspace access denials through server logs — bundled into this epic because the denial audit entry is written in the same artifact-request validation/denial handler the P0 requirements build, and the HLR text already fixes the channel (server logs); a split would modify the same handler twice.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":69283,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcm