{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8016f960d361c4de","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E004\n3\ttitle: Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n5\tcomponent: MLflow\n6\tteam: MLflow Team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E005\n12\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: rewrite\n15\t  fallback_approach: If the flags cannot be made composable without breaking the initialization\n16\t    sequence for either flag used individually, restructure the artifacts-only server\n17\t    initialization path per the feasibility findings before implementing workspace\n18\t    validation; workspace-aware artifacts-only serving is deferred until the restructure\n19\t    lands.\n20\tai_signals:\n21\t  change_specificity: 1\n22\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n23\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n24\t  existing_foundation: 1\n25\t  open_questions: -1\n26\t  external_dependency: 1\n27\t  human_process_gates: -1\n28\t  repo_access: 1\n29\t  architecture_claims: 1\n30\timplementation_type: null\n31\tinvestigation_signals: null\n32\tai_implementability: High\n33\tai_implementability_score: 5\n34\tjira_key: null\n35\tbranch: null\n36\t---\n37\t# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n38\t\n39\t## Description\n40\t\n41\tThis epic makes the MLflow tracking server's `--artifacts-only` mode composable with the existing `--enable-workspaces` flag, so the artifacts-only server initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests against tenant boundaries. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`, validates each artifact request against workspace membership, denies unauthorized requests, and fails closed when the workspace store is unreachable. Deployments that do not set `--enable-workspaces` retain the current artifacts-only behavior. This epic depends on the resolution of the workspace-aware artifacts-only feasibility questions (initialization approach, artifact path resolution) and on the plugin workspace-resolution capability being verified for the artifacts-only context.\n42\t\n43\t## Scope\n44\t\n45\t- Allow `--artifacts-only` to be combined with `--enable-workspaces` without a new server flag: when both are set, require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` (the server fails to start with a clear error if it is missing) and initialize only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests. Tracking stores and model registry stores stay uninitialized; non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n46\t- For each artifact request, resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment (artifacts are stored under experiment-scoped paths, e.g. `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure — without prescribing Kubernetes API mechanics. Initial artifact path resolution scope: the S3 artifact store (the standard RHOAI configuration); limitations for non-standard artifact roots are documented.\n47\t- Deny requests that fail workspace validation with HTTP 403 and an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions; deny requests with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable when the workspace store is unreachable (fail-closed).\n48\t- Preserve current behavior when either flag is absent: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` serves artifacts without workspace validation; single-tenant deployments and existing API contracts, CLI arguments, and configuration keys are unchanged in meaning.\n49\t- Audit logging for workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs (P2 requirement, bundled here because the denial log entry is written in the same artifact-request validation handler).\n50\t- Measure the per-request latency overhead of workspace validation and apply short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions consistent with the tracking server's existing auth-path caching patterns if the measurement warrants it; otherwise document the deferral rationale.\n51\t- Update incidental documentation (flag usage, limitations for non-standard artifact roots) that falls out of this change.\n52\t\n53\t## Acceptance Criteria\n54\t\n55\t- Given the artifacts-only instance started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://` and a user who is a member of workspace A, when the user requests an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A, then the artifact is served — measured by HTTP 200 with correct artifact content.\n56\t- Given the workspace-aware artifacts-only instance and a user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B, when the user requests an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B, then the request is denied — measured by HTTP 403 with an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n57\t- Given `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, when any authenticated user requests an artifact, then the artifact is served without workspace validation — measured by HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership (current behavior preserved).\n58\t- Given the workspace-aware artifacts-only instance running with the workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable, when a user requests an artifact, then the request is denied — measured by HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable (fail-closed).\n59\t- The server fails to start with a clear error when `--enable-workspaces` is combined with `--artifacts-only` without an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`.\n60\t- No existing API contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys are removed or changed in meaning.\n61\t- Workspace access denials in artifact serving are recorded in the server logs.\n62\t- The per-request workspace validation overhead is measured, and caching is either implemented or explicitly deferred with a recorded rationale.\n63\t\n64\t## HLR Traceability\n65\t\n66\t- HLR-1 (P0): `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` — this epic.\n67\t- HLR-2 (P0): artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 — this epic.\n68\t- HLR-3 (P0): fail-closed when the workspace store is unreachable — this epic.\n69\t- HLR-4 (P0): single-tenant / non-workspace deployments retain current behavior — this epic.\n70\t- HLR-8 (P2, bundled): audit logging for workspace access denials through server logs — bundled into this epic because the denial audit entry is written in the same artifact-request validation/denial handler the P0 requirements build, and the HLR text already fixes the channel (server logs); a split would modify the same handler twice.\n71\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c859964578464b6","agent_id":"a0a6494381c67b2ac","session_id":"93c9eca1-ec44-4ff5-8343-c9e3bc7bdf0f","uuid":"72e40a8c-41c7-41eb-8500-9a95118b7469","timestamp":"2026-10-26T17:38:23.564Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8016f960d361c4de","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
