{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-af553fc5ca82f1cb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: Completeness\n9\t  description: 'Summary Investigation Gating section says the Helm second-Deployment\n10\t    assumption is captured as an AC on E006, but E006 states it only in Scope (bullet\n11\t    1), not as a distinct acceptance criterion. Correction: either add an explicit\n12\t    AC to E006 (chart extended with a second Deployment without structural changes\n13\t    to the chart architecture) or reword the summary to say it is captured in Scope.'\n14\terror: null\n15\t---\n16\t## Review Summary\n17\t\n18\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n19\tRecommendation: accept\n20\t\n21\tContract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py RHAISTRAT-8009` exited 0 — body sections, canonical component names, priority inheritance, parallelism rules, and Rule 11 coverage all pass). Triage consistency: `triage: null` (full decomposition), so the 6-epic multi-epic output is expected and not penalized.\n22\t\n23\tThe decomposition is a faithful translation of the strategy: three genuine investigations gating three implementation epics plus one conditional branch, all 8 HLRs traced, Staff Engineer overrides (no new flag, split deployment not config toggle, no SSAR mechanics) applied throughout, and the operator/server coupling correctly expressed as an acceptance-criterion gate rather than a DAG edge.\n24\t\n25\t## Criterion Details\n26\t\n27\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n28\tAll 8 HLRs from the strategy map to epics: HLR-1..4 (P0) → E004; HLR-5, HLR-6 (P1) → E006 with the conditional server-side portion of HLR-6 in BRANCH-B-E007; HLR-7 (P1) → E005; HLR-8 (P2) → E004. The traceability matrix is present and each epic has an HLR Traceability section. Priority inheritance is correct: E005 is raised to P0 because it blocks the P0 server epic E004 (Health Warning 6 documents this as inheritance working, not an inversion — correct). E006 is P1 and blocks no P0 work. HLR-8 bundling into E004 is recorded directly in the matrix row with a `(bundled)` flag and inline rationale (same denial handler, channel fixed by the HLR text, not separately schedulable) — the compliant bundling shape, not priority collapse.\n29\t\n30\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n31\tDiagram and frontmatter are mutually consistent: every edge in the `graph TD` diagram (dependency → dependent) has a matching entry in the target epic's `dependencies`, and vice versa (E001/E002/E003 have none; E004 → {E001, E005}; E005 → {E002}; E006 → {E003}; BRANCH-B-E007 → {E003}). No circular dependencies, no edges to nonexistent epics. No main-plan epic lists the branch epic in its dependencies. Each investigation edge is a true gate with `gated_by` set on the dependent and present in its direct `dependencies` (E004/E001, E005/E002, E006/E003, branch/E003). The E005 → E004 edge is a justified framework→consumer dependency (the server's artifact validation builds against the plugin's workspace-resolution capability — a code/API dependency, not a config reference). The deliberately absent E006 ← E004/E005 edge is the rule-compliant shape: the operator renders Deployment args referencing the flag combination, which is a configuration reference expressed as the end-to-end AC on E006, not a DAG edge. Branch parallelism with E004 (same repo, different areas) is correctly left unlinked per the coordination-risk rule. Critical path length 3 (E002 → E005 → E004) matches the declared `critical_path_length: 3` and is appropriate for an M-sized strategy. No un-captured data/artifact dependencies found in epic content.\n32\t\n33\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n34\tThe canonical component list contains only `MLflow`; the decomposition documents the mapping (operator and plugins work logged under the canonical `MLflow` component while epic boundaries follow the strategy's three repo surfaces — server, plugins, operator). All epics are MLflow Team, matching the strategy's impacted-teams statement, so no team boundary violation. No scope duplication: E004 (artifacts-only serving) and BRANCH-B-E007 (tracking-server delegation) touch different areas of the server; E006's operator-side routing and the branch's server-side delegation are complementary halves split on the repo boundary. No work-product type mixing. Size: E006 (~2-3 sprints) exceeds the ~2-week guideline but is the defensible shape — one logical change to the operator's chart/reconciler, kept whole with a recorded estimate, why-not-split rationale, and a concrete conditional re-split trigger in Health Warnings; recorded as a watch item, not docked. E004 (1-2 sprints) is within range.\n35\t\n36\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n37\tAll three Investigations genuinely change downstream structure: E001 determines the server initialization approach (rewrite of E004 on failure), E002 determines whether E005 is verification-only or refactoring, E003 selects the routing mechanism that shapes E006 and activates/removes the conditional branch. Each has downstream dependents. All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts. Every epic with `gated_by` has a complete `gate_failure_impact` (action + fallback_approach); the branch epic uses `action: remove` with correct outcome-A semantics. Every Investigation listed in `dependencies` has `gated_by` set on the dependent epic. No misclassifications.\n38\t\n39\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n40\tType-appropriate signal sets throughout: the three Investigation epics carry `investigation_signals` (5-signal rubric), the four Implementation epics (including the branch) carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric); no crossover. Signal values are consistent with strategy content and the rationale files: e.g. E005's `change_specificity: 0` and `repo_access: 0` are justified by the unresolved plugin-coupling scope and the absence of a documented plugin repo (accurate — `.context/architecture-context/` has `mlflow.md` and `mlflow-operator.md` but no plugins file); the branch epic's `pattern_precedent: 0` / `adapter_pattern: 0` reflect that no split-server delegation pattern exists in the current deployment. `architecture_claims` is calibrated: E004 and E006 score +1 with rationales citing the actual context files (mlflow.md Dockerfile.konflux CMD; mlflow-operator.md Helm-in-operator and conditional CRD discovery), both of which exist in `.context`. Frontmatter values match the rationale tables. No arithmetic or threshold checks performed (pipeline-computed).\n41\t\n42\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n43\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy — the strategy's six proposed acceptance criteria appear essentially verbatim across E004 (ACs 1-4 → the 200/403/200/503 scenarios) and E006 (ACs 5-6 → distinct Deployment resources and the rendered container command). Backwards-compatibility AC present on E004 (\"no existing API contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys removed or changed in meaning\") and on the branch epic (no behavior change when delegation not configured). No replacement/konflux/docs-authoring epics exist, so no rule-mandated ACs of those kinds apply. No externally-controlled preconditions misfiled as ACs — E006's end-to-end AC conditions on what the epic itself verifies against a sibling epic's build, which is the prescribed AC-gate shape. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID; all cross-references use full IDs in frontmatter only.\n44\t\n45\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n46\tThe full strategy scope is covered: flag composition, workspace validation with 403, fail-closed 503, single-tenant preservation, audit logging (bundled), operator split topology with Service/HTTPRoute/kube-rbac-proxy/CR validation, routing (with conditional branch), and plugin resolution. All four strategy risks are carried forward (path-resolution complexity → E001