{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a589d0618903b4f9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8009\n11\t\n12\tWorkspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments: make `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces`, and have the MLflow operator deploy a dedicated artifacts-only instance in a split-server topology.\n13\t\n14\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n15\t\n16\tThe Staff Engineer / SME Input section is non-empty and takes precedence. Three directives were applied; the strategy body already incorporates them, and the decomposition follows them:\n17\t\n18\t1. **No new flag** — no `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware` flag and no `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field appear anywhere in the epic scopes; all server work combines the existing `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` flags.\n19\t2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle** — the operator epic is scoped as a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute plus artifact traffic routing from the main tracking deployment, not a single CR field with Helm values propagation.\n20\t3. **SSAR detail is over-specified** — epic scopes state that workspace authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure without dictating Kubernetes API mechanics; no epic mandates SelfSubjectAccessReview or any specific Kubernetes authorization primitive. The kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` setup are explicitly unchanged.\n21\t\n22\t## Component Mappings\n23\t\n24\tThe canonical component list (`.context/rhai-components.txt`) contains `MLflow` but has no separate entries for the MLflow operator or the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins package. Mappings used (logged per the component-name constraint):\n25\t\n26\t| Strategy component | Canonical Jira component | Note |\n27\t|----|----|----|\n28\t| MLflow | MLflow | verbatim |\n29\t| MLflow Operator | MLflow | closest canonical name; work spans the mlflow-operator repo (Helm chart templates, reconciler) |\n30\t| mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | MLflow | closest canonical name; work spans the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins package (auth plugin workspace resolution) |\n31\t\n32\tEpic boundaries still follow the strategy's component/repo boundaries (three distinct implementation epics); only the Jira `component` field is the canonical name.\n33\t\n34\t## Epic List\n35\t\n36\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n37\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n38\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Investigation: workspace-aware artifacts-only serving feasibility in the MLflow server | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n39\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Investigation: mlflow-kubernetes-plugins workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n40\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Investigation: artifact traffic routing mechanism for the MLflow split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n41\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n42\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | mlflow-kubernetes-plugins workspace resolution for the artifacts-only server context | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n43\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E006 | MLflow operator split-server topology for dedicated artifacts-only serving | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n44\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007 | Tracking server artifact traffic delegation to the dedicated artifacts-only instance | Implementation (branch B) | MLflow Team | P1 |\n45\t\n46\tBranch epic `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007` is a conditional alternative attached to the routing investigation (E003), not a standalone main-plan node; `epic_count` counts the 6 main-plan epics.\n47\t\n48\t## Dependency DAG\n49\t\n50\t```mermaid\n51\tgraph TD\n52\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: server feasibility\"] --> E004[\"E004 Workspace-aware artifacts-only server\"]\n53\t    E002[\"E002 Investigation: plugin coupling\"] --> E005[\"E005 Plugin workspace resolution\"]\n54\t    E005[\"E005 Plugin workspace resolution\"] --> E004[\"E004 Workspace-aware artifacts-only server\"]\n55\t    E003[\"E003 Investigation: routing mechanism\"] --> E006[\"E006 Operator split-server topology\"]\n56\t    E003 -.-> BRANCHB[\"BRANCH-B-E007 Tracking server delegation (conditional)\"]\n57\t```\n58\t\n59\tArrows point from dependency to dependent (`X --> Y` means X must complete before Y starts). The dashed edge is a conditional branch attachment, not a main-plan edge.\n60\t\n61\t## DAG Justification\n62\t\n63\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n64\t|------|------|-----------|\n65\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The plugin-coupling investigation determines whether the plugin epic is verification-only or requires refactoring workspace resolution into an independent initialization path — a true gate (`rewrite`). |\n66\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The feasibility investigation determines the server initialization approach and the artifact-path resolution strategy — a true gate (`rewrite`). |\n67\t| E005 → E004 | Rules 12/13 (framework → consumer) | The plugin supplies the workspace-resolution library that the server's artifact-request validation builds against; the consuming server implementation waits for the plugin capability to be verified/extended for the artifacts-only context. |\n68\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The routing investigation selects the mechanism (gateway path-based routing vs tracking-server delegation) that shapes the operator's HTTPRoute design and routing configuration — a true gate (`rewrite`). |\n69\t| E003 ⇢ BRANCH-B-E007 | Rule 3, conditional branch | The branch epic exists only under outcome B (tracking-server delegation required); bounded outcome, conditional decomposition branch. |\n70\t\n71\tDeliberately **absent** edges:\n72\t\n73\t- **E006 (operator) is not gated by E004/E005 (server/plugin work)** — different repos; the operator renders Deployment args referencing the flag combination, which is a configuration reference, not a build-against artifact (Rule 13 exclusion). The coupling is expressed as an end-to-end acceptance criterion on E006 (integration verification against the workspace-aware artifacts-only server build), never as a DAG edge.\n74\t- **E001 ∥ E002 ∥ E003** — three independent investigations over different unknowns (server init/path resolution, plugin coupling, routing mechanism); no shared deliverable, so they are not combined.\n75\t- **BRANCH-B-E007 ∥ E004** — same repo, different areas of the tracking server (delegation config vs artifacts-only serving); merge conflicts are a coordination risk, not a dependency (Rule 15).\n76\t\n77\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n78\t\n79\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n80\t|-----|----------|---------|\n81\t| HLR-1: `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` + `--workspace-store-uri` | P0 | E004 |\n82\t| HLR-2: artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | P0 | E004 |\n83\t| HLR-3: fail-closed when workspace store unreachable | P0 | E004 |\n84\t| HLR-4: single-tenant / non-workspace deployments retain current behavior | P0 | E004 |\n85\t| HLR-5: operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute | P1 | E006 |\n86\t| HLR-6: main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance | P1 | E006; BRANCH-B-E007 (conditional server-side portion under routing outcome B) |\n87\t| HLR-7: auth plugin workspace resolution works without tracking store initialization | P1 | E005 |\n88\t| HLR-8 (P2): audit logging for workspace access denials via server logs | P2 | E004 (bundled) — the denial audit log entry is written in the same artifact-request validation/denial handler the P0 HLRs build, and the HLR text already fixes the channel (server logs), so it is not separately schedulable; a split would modify the same handler twice |\n89\t\n90\tAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n91\t\n92\t## Investigation Gating Summary\n93\t\n94\t| Investigation | Gates | gate_failure_impact |\n95\t|----|----|----|\n96\t| E001 (server feasibility) | E004 | rewrite — if the flags cannot compose without breaking either flag's initialization sequence, the artifacts-only server initialization path must be restructured before implementation |\n97\t| E002 (plugin coupling) | E005 | rewrite — if workspace resolution is coupled to tracking store initialization, the plugin epic expands to factoring resolution into an independent initialization path; if uncoupled, it reduces to verification and test coverage |\n98\t| E003 (routing mechanism) | E006 (+ branch selection) | rewrite — if gateway path-based routing is insufficient, the operator's routing design changes and the conditional tracking-server delegation branch activates |\n99\t\n100\tOpen questions **not** promoted to Investigations (with disposition):\n101\t\n102\t- **Latency overhead / caching at launch (OQ1, PM/Engineering)** — does not change which epics exist or what they do; caching is an in-epic mitigation within the server epic's scope. Captured as a measurement AC on E004 plus a Health Warning.\n103\t- **Audit telemetry channel (OQ4, PM/Security)** — HLR-8 already fixes server logs as the channel; a different channel would change implementation detail inside the same epic, not epic structure. Captured as an AC on E004 plus a Health Warning.\n104\t- **Helm chart supports a second Deployment (assumption)** — implementation detail for the operator epic; captured as an AC on E006 (chart extension without structural change to the chart architecture).\n105\t- **Single-tenant deployments do not set `--enable-workspaces` (assumption)** — backwards-compatibility AC on E004 (HLR-4) and verified within E001's scope.\n106\t\n107\t## Health Warnings\n108\t\n109\t1. **Epic size — E006 (operator split-server topology)**: ~2-3 sprints by the strategy's effort arithmetic, above the ~2-week guideline. Kept whole because the dedicated Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute, flag propagation, and traffic routing are one logical change to the operator's Helm chart and reconciler — splitting instance deployment from traffic routing would render and verify the same chart surface twice and leave the CR surface half-defined. Re-split trigger: if the routing investigation selects the tracking-server delegation outcome (activating the conditional branch), or the chart work exceeds the strategy's 3-sprint upper estimate, split into a dedicated-instance deployment epic and an artifact-traffic-routing epic.\n110\t2. **Epic size — E004 (workspace-aware artifacts-only server)**: 1-2 sprints of server-side work plus its share of integration testing. Kept whole because flag composition, workspace validation, fail-closed handling, and denial audit logging are one change to the same artifact-request handling path. Re-split trigger: if the audit-channel decision expands beyond server logs to a telemetry integration (OTLP / Kubernetes audit log), or the workspace-validation work exceeds the strategy's 2-sprint upper estimate, split the audit-logging scope into a separate follow-up epic.\n111\t3. **Performance NFR has no measurable target** — neither the RFE nor the architecture context specifies an acceptable per-request latency overhead. The latency open question is captured as a measurement AC on E004; risk that PM requires launch-blocking caching after measurement (the strategy's risk mitigation already prescribes short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions).\n112\t4. **Audit channel ambiguity** — the P2 HLR specifies server logs while the open question lists OTLP and the Kubernetes audit log as alternatives (owner PM/Security). If another channel is selected, E004's bundled audit scope grows; see the E004 re-split trigger above.\n113\t5. **Pending human process gates** — Architecture Review, ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, and other prerequisites are all \"Pending human review\" at the strategy level. These are external to any single epic; implementation epics carry `human_process_gates = -1` accordingly.\n114\t6. **Priority note (informational)** — E005 is P1 by its own HLR-7 but is scheduled P0 by priority inheritance because it blocks the P0 server epic (E004). This is inheritance working as intended, not an inversion.\n115\t\n116\t## Tiered Delivery\n117\t\n118\tNot applicable. All dependencies (workspace store, auth plugin, Gateway API, kube-rbac-proxy) are internal and exist per the strategy's dependency table; there is no external dependency with uncertain timing, so no Tier 1 / Tier 2 split is needed.\n119\t\n120\t## Conditional Branches\n121\t\n122\tThe routing investigation (E003) has two bounded outcomes:\n123\t\n124\t- **Outcome A — gateway path-based routing suffices**: the main plan stands as written; E006 implements HTTPRoute-based routing of artifact API traffic to the dedicated instance. No additional epic.\n125\t- **Outcome B — tracking-server delegation required**: the main plan stands, plus conditional branch epic `RHAISTRAT-8009-BRANCH-B-E007` (Tracking server artifact traffic delegation to the dedicated artifacts-only instance), gated by E003 with `gate_failure_impact.action = remove` (if outcome A holds, the branch epic is removed).\n126\t\n127\tBranch epics are excluded from the main-plan critical path. Main-plan critical path: E002 → E005 → E004 (length 3).\n128\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b619a9aaa03fbefc","agent_id":"a1478beee049b97f9","session_id":"93c9eca1-ec44-4ff5-8343-c9e3bc7bdf0f","uuid":"0158f8db-cafd-45ba-a6f4-6003cfd8fca8","timestamp":"2026-10-26T17:42:35.898Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a589d0618903